\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Status of the five criteria}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{For the binary tetrahedral datum of
[the finite spinor identities](fdip-00A0), the 5 criteria
of [the compatibility datum](fdip-009Z) have the following
status. Exactness, the selector effect and reference recovery
are proved. The declared maps and the spinor separation are
conditional. The unresolved part of the declared maps is the
absence of the larger classical correspondences. That absence
is not a nonexistence theorem.}

\p{The worked test in the background plan was left multiplication
of #{2T} on the spinor module #{\mathbb H} of #{Cl^+(3)}.
The test published in \ref{fdip-00A0} realifies the
right-#{\mathbb C} quaternion spinor to #{\mathbb H_R} before
forming the sum with the rotation summand
#{\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H}. Each value #{T(g)} is an
invertible real endomorphism of
#{\mathbb H_R\oplus\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H},
##{T(g)=\rho_s^{\mathbb R}(g)\oplus\tau(g)
\in\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb R}(\mathbb H_R\oplus\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H).}
Left multiplication on #{\mathbb H_R\cong\mathbb R^4} is the
realification of #{\rho_s}. The rotation summand #{\tau} is
additional.}

\proof{
\p{Exactness is proved. The group is the Hurwitz model of
#{2T}, and the coefficient field is #{\mathbb R}. The
right-#{\mathbb C} spinor realifies to #{\mathbb H_R}, and
the rotation summand is #{\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H}, so
#{T(g)} is an invertible element of
#{\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb R}(\mathbb H_R\oplus\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H)}.
Realification carries #{\rho_s(-1)=-I_2} to #{-I} on
#{\mathbb H_R}, and #{\tau(-1)=I_3}, so
#{T(-1)=-I_{\mathbb H_R}\oplus I_{\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H}}.
The selector of \ref{fdip-00A0} is the identity on the first
summand and zero on the second. The graph action is the trivial
action declared with these representations.}

\p{Declare the ordered real basis #{(1,i,-j,k)} of
#{\mathbb H_R}, the real and imaginary parts, in that order, of
the ordered complex basis #{(1,-j)} under right multiplication
by #{i}, and declare the ordered basis #{(i,j,k)} of
#{\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H}. In this concatenated ordered
basis the matrix of #{T(g)} lies in #{GL_7(\mathbb R)}. The
matrix of #{T(-1)} is #{-I_4\oplus I_3}. The complex block
form #{P=I_2\oplus 0} in \ref{fdip-00A0} realifies to
#{I_4\oplus 0}, the matrix of the selector.}

\p{The declared maps are conditional. The two maps that are
constructed are #{\pi:SU(2)\to SO(3)} with kernel
#{\{1,-1\}}, and #{2T\to A_4} with the same kernel. Both
are the finite identities of \ref{fdip-00A0}. No homomorphism,
kernel, cover or branching rule is constructed here for
#{Sp(3,1)}, #{SU(3,1)}, #{SU(3,2)}, #{SL(4,\mathbb R)},
or a Hessian extension. Those comparisons remain open.
Their absence is not a nonexistence theorem.}

\p{Separation is conditional. The spinor representation
sends the quaternion #{-1} to #{-I_2}, while the rotation
representation sends it to #{I_3}. The [representation datum](fdip-007H)
already separates an internal factor #{1\otimes\rho} from
an action on the spinor factor, and the
[covariant Dirac operator](fdip-009N) requires Clifford
compatibility before a binary rotation group can act as a
gauge transformation. The quaternion multiplication table
of \ref{fdip-00A0} does not provide that compatibility law.
The gravitational comparison available here is only the
quotient #{SU(2)\to SO(3)}. A larger gravitational
identification is unresolved.}

\p{The selector effect is proved for the operator named in
the datum. With #{P} the identity on #{\mathbb H_R} and zero
on #{\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H},
##{PT(g)P=\rho_s^{\mathbb R}(g)\oplus 0.}
This typed projection retains the realified spinor summand and
removes the rotation summand. In the ordered basis declared
above, its matrix is that block. The same idempotent is not a
link variable of the covariant Dirac operator, so this identity
makes no claim about the kernel of that operator.}

\p{Reference recovery is proved for the declared comparison
#{C}. That comparison is #{\tau:2T\to A_4\subset SO(3)},
the tetrahedral rotation representation. The kernel is
#{\{1,-1\}} and the image has order #{12}, as proved in
\ref{fdip-00A0}. The spinor fiber does not factor through
that quotient. That caveat is stated under separation, above.
It does not leave the recovery of this rotation representation
unproved. The datum claims no lattice measure,
continuum quantum field, or laboratory observable beyond
this finite rotation representation.}
}
